   "id": "co_torrey_s_peak_north_face_aka_tuning_fork",
   "area_id": "co_torrey_s_peak",
   "name": "North Face (aka Tuning Fork)",
   "grade": "Mod. Snow R",
   "discipline": "mountaineering",
   "overview": "Easily spotted by drivers on the interstate near Bakersville, a wide ribbon of snow dropping about 1,000 m, also known as the Tuning Fork. To ski it, take the Grizzly Creek road to the Grays Peak trailhead, climb by skin or boot, cross the Grays-Torreys saddle, and gain the summit by the southeast ridge. Descend the ridge toward Grizzly Peak until the line appears; loose talus leads down to snow, with nowhere to stop and click in. A few easy turns come before the steep choke, 40+ degrees, that sets the difficulty. Booting up the face is a good workout, but rockfall makes that unwise.",
   "description": null,
   "approach": "Use the Grays Peak trailhead and follow the trail toward the Grays and Torreys route; in winter the road is gated at the I-70 exit, making for a long day. Roughly a mile up, at the Grays Peak sign, turn west down Grizzly Gulch to reach the Tuning Fork, a long strip of snow on Torreys' northwest face, visible from the interstate. The standard route to the saddle and summit crosses avalanche paths, and the face can be climbed directly as a difficult Class 2 snow climb. These are steep snow slopes in avalanche terrain, so check the avalanche forecast first.",
